commit d6614025fa1f6133e34c4fe0cea2815d672d8742
Author: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 21 22:30:43 2026 +0400

    Load weight files a safetensors index leaves out (#562)
    
    * Load weight files a safetensors index leaves out
    
    Loading has honored `model.safetensors.index.json` since #408, which keeps
    sidecar checkpoints such as `mtp.safetensors` out of the main model. Two
    checkpoint layouts are not served by that rule:
    
    - `jinaai/jina-reranker-v3-mlx` ships its reranking head in
      `projector.safetensors` and never names it in the index, so
      `JinaRerankerModel` loaded without `projector.linear1.weight` and failed
      `update(parameters:verify:[.all])` (#560).
    - Several `mlx-community/Qwen3-VL-*` uploads kept the index of their
      unquantized source repo, so it names shards the repo does not ship and
      loading finds no weights at all (#554).
    
    Add `BaseLanguageModel.additionalWeightFiles`, defaulting to empty, for a
    model to name the sidecars its checkpoint's index omits, and declare
    `projector.safetensors` on `JinaRerankerModel`. Those files load after the
    indexed ones, so the index still wins for any tensor both provide, and names
    that are not present are ignored.
    
    Fall back to every safetensors file in the directory when the index names a
    file the directory does not contain. A complete index still excludes
    unrelated sidecars exactly as before.
    
    * Cover the stale-index fallback with an integration test
    
    `mlx-community/Qwen3-VL-4B-Instruct-4bit` is the checkpoint from #554: it
    ships one `model.safetensors` while its index names two shards it does not
    ship. Nothing in the suite loaded it, which is why the loader change has no
    end-to-end coverage otherwise. Pin the revision, since the mismatch is a
    property of this upload and a re-quantized revision would stop covering the
    fallback, and generate a few tokens after loading: the weights have to be the
    right ones, not merely present.
    
    * Select weight files by convention, not by trusting the index
    
    Following the reference implementation rather than patching around the index.
    `mlx_lm.utils.load_model` selects weight files with `glob("model*.safetensors")`
    at the top level and never reads `model.safetensors.index.json`, which is why
    every checkpoint in #554 and #560 loads there and fails here.
    
    Two consequences for the loader:
    
    - Only the top level of the model directory is considered. The checkpoint that
      motivated #408, `mlx-community/Qwen3.5-4B-OptiQ-4bit`, keeps its auxiliary
      weights in `optiq/`, so the recursive walk was the reason they were loaded at
      all -- and that failure is silent, since a stray tensor whose name `sanitize`
      rewrites corrupts generation instead of raising. `ParoQuantLoader` already
      restricts itself to the top level for the same reason.
    - When the index names a file the directory does not contain, fall back to the
      conventional `model*.safetensors` names (then `weight*.safetensors`, then
      whatever is present) rather than to every file found. Loading everything
      would reintroduce exactly the corruption above on any repo that is both
      stale-indexed and OptiQ-shaped.
    
    A complete index still wins, so a repo that ships both a consolidated file and
    shards keeps loading precisely.
    
    Move the sidecar declaration to an optional `AdditionalWeightFilesProviding`
    protocol, matching `ModelConversionMetadataProvider` next to it, so
    `BaseLanguageModel` does not grow a requirement most models do not use.
    
    Add `ModelConfiguration.weightFileSelection` for the other half of #554: a
    caller that knows a checkpoint's index omits weights no registered model
    declares can ask for every file present. It carries through
    `ResolvedModelConfiguration` into the LLM, VLM, embedder, and MTP drafter
    factories.
    
    * Read the projector from both packagings of the Jina reranker
    
    `jinaai/jina-reranker-v3` and `jinaai/jina-reranker-v3-mlx` are one model
    packaged two ways, and the same model class has to read both:
    
    - the upstream repo has no index, keeps the projector in its single
      `model.safetensors`, and stores it as `nn.Sequential(Linear, ReLU, Linear)`,
      so the layers arrive as `projector.0.weight` and `projector.2.weight`
    - the MLX repo carries an index that names neither the projector nor its file,
      moves those layers into `projector.safetensors`, and renames them to bare
      `linear1.weight` and `linear2.weight`
    
    File selection already handled the first: a declared file that is not present
    is ignored, and with no index the conventional `model*.safetensors` names carry
    the projector in. The tensor names did not, so `sanitize` accepts both
    spellings.  Which files to load and what the tensors are called are separate
    questions, and only the first is packaging the loader can reason about.
    
    Cover both spellings in unit tests, and run the upstream checkpoint through the
    same integration assertions as the MLX one: the weights are the same bfloat16,
    so it has to reproduce the reference scores from `rerank.py`.

