commit 7871b09b2eda7500bc2acad51125ebd772cbaffe
Author: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 00:43:02 2026 +0400

    Tool call parser hardening (#531)
    
    * Keep decoded 0 and 1 as integers in tool-call arguments
    
    `JSONValue.from` tested `as? Bool` before `as? Int`. `JSONSerialization`
    boxes every scalar as `NSNumber`, and an `NSNumber` holding 0 or 1 bridges
    to `Bool` successfully, so any argument value decoded as JSON had its 0s
    and 1s rewritten as booleans: `{"limit": 1}` arrived as `{"limit": true}`.
    
    This reached every parser that decodes a value as JSON rather than through
    `Codable` — Gemma, Pythonic, XML function, GLM4, Kimi K2, MiniMax M2,
    Llama 3, Mistral and ATEM — and broke tool execution whenever a schema
    declared the parameter an integer.
    
    Key the boolean case on the boxed type instead of on a cast that a number
    also satisfies. Numbers keep their existing classification.
    
    * Split tool-call arguments with a shared structural scanner
    
    The Pythonic and Gemma dialects separate arguments with punctuation, so
    both have to know whether a comma, colon or bracket is a separator or part
    of a value. Both answered that with plain index searches, which truncated
    any value containing the delimiter:
    
    - Pythonic extracted the call with a lazy `\[(\w+)\((.*?)\)\]` regex, so
      `notify(note='a)]b')` lost everything from `)]` onward and kept a stray
      quote (#493). `parseMultiple`, which backs `parseEOS`, had the same flaw.
    - Gemma read each value up to the first comma and the call up to the last
      brace, so `call:search{filters:{"city":"Paris","limit":1}}` truncated the
      object and turned its tail into an invented `"limit"` argument.
    
    Introduce `StructuredTextScanner`, which walks text while treating quoted
    spans, marker-delimited spans (Gemma's `<|"|>`) and bracket nesting as
    opaque, and reports only structural positions with their depth. Both
    parsers, and the LFM2 bracket scan in `ToolCallProcessor`, now share it:
    three hand-rolled scanners collapse into one that is tested directly.
    
    Behavior is otherwise preserved, including the requirement that a bracketed
    Pythonic list be balanced before any call in it is executed.
    
    * Infer the tool-call dialect from the checkpoint
    
    A checkpoint whose architecture declares no `toolCallFormat` fell back to
    the JSON parser, so a model that emits any other dialect had every call
    pass through as plain text. The chat template is what teaches the model to
    emit a dialect, so it identifies that dialect even for an unknown model.
    
    Add `ToolCallFormat.inferred(fromChatTemplate:)`, mirroring the signature
    table in mlx-lm's `_infer_tool_parser`, and `ToolCallFormat(toolParserType:)`
    for checkpoints that name their parser outright. Both are pure and tested
    directly; a small reader supplies them from `tokenizer_config.json` or a
    `chat_template.jinja` sidecar, since the tokenizer exposes only whether a
    template exists and not its text.
    
    The factories consult this last, after an explicit configuration value, a
    registered resolver and the model's own declaration, so no model that
    already resolves a format changes behavior. Protocols with token-level
    framing (Harmony, Onyx) are deliberately not inferred: they are selected by
    the models that own them, and guessing them from text would reroute the
    whole response protocol rather than one payload shape.
    
    * Support Python literals in Pythonic tool calls
    
    Parse single-quoted collections and Python scalar spellings without a schema while preserving declared schema types.
    
    Closes #494.
    
    * Align tool-call parsing with selected templates
    
    Prefer the tool_use template when a checkpoint publishes named chat templates. Reconcile its inferred dialect with model declarations so Hermes refines the heuristic Llama 3 parser to framed JSON while compatible supersets such as Qwen 3.5 and full response protocols remain selected.

