commit 4ffa64deea2e88ee32bd3764fc3c10ac80439514
Author: Charlie Le <charlie_le@apple.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 12 15:12:31 2026 -0700

    Add Muse-Glimmer, a 30B agentic multimodal model, with ATEM tool calling (#523)
    
    * Add Muse-Glimmer, a 30B multimodal model with a native-resolution ViT
    
    Ports mlx_vlm.models.muse_glimmer: a 52-layer dense text transformer paired
    with a 50-layer native-resolution vision tower, registered as "muse_glimmer"
    with its "MuseGlimmerProcessor".
    
    Several pieces are not standard and are where a port bug would hide, so each
    is commented at its definition:
    
    - Full-attention layers get no positional encoding. `layer_rope_theta` is 0
      for them and 500000 on the sliding layers, so RoPE is skipped entirely
      every fourth layer.
    - Attention output is gated: `output * sigmoid(gate_proj(x))`, where the gate
      reads the layer input rather than the attention result. Note the checkpoint
      key collides with `mlp.gate_proj` at a different path.
    - Queries are scaled by `qk_scale_factor` (3.87) *in addition to* the usual
      1/sqrt(head_dim), after a parameterless QK norm. Effective scale is
      3.87/sqrt(128).
    - Block norms scale by `1 + weight` (the checkpoint stores a scale centered
      at zero) and alternate between two eps values: rms_norm_eps on the two
      "pre" norms, the much smaller post_norm_eps on the two "post" ones. The
      final norm is a plain RMSNorm instead.
    - `embed_norm`, `qk_norm` and `perception_emb_norm` have no learnable scale
      and so are absent from the checkpoint. They are modelled as parameterless
      modules to keep the module tree matching the weight keys exactly.
    - The ViT does window attention by permuting tokens into contiguous windows
      and running chunked SDPA, so no banded mask is ever materialized. The
      window side reuses pos_emb_height (32), which looks accidental upstream but
      is what every published checkpoint was trained with.
    - The learned 32x32 position table is bilinearly resampled onto the actual
      patch grid as a four-corner gather, including validity masks that zero
      out-of-range corners rather than clamping them.
    - 2D RoPE and the 2x2 patch merge are hand-rolled: the merge puts the channel
      axis before the two merge axes, so each merged vector is channel-major.
    - Head dim is 96 in the ViT, off the fused SDPA path, so q/k/v are padded to
      128 and sliced back. Zero padding contributes nothing to any dot product,
      so this is exact.
    - The patch embedder is a Linear, not a convolution: the processor emits
      pre-flattened patches in raster order, laid out (temporal, channels, ph, pw).
    
    The 4-bit checkpoint is only partially quantized -- the whole vision tower
    stays bf16 -- which needs no special handling, since the existing load path
    keys quantization off the presence of `.scales`.
    
    Verified against the Python reference rather than by reading output, because
    a wrong permutation here produces fluent, confident, wrong text. Every
    permutation and index computation is compared to reference values as exact
    integers. On the full model, top-1 and top-5 at the final prompt position
    agree exactly and greedy decoding tracks the reference token for token until
    a genuine near-tie, where the top-2 margin is smaller than the two
    implementations' logit disagreement. Deep-ViT per-position cosine is loose in
    both directions: the reference run in fp32 diverges from its own bf16 output
    further than this port does.
    
    Video input and the <atem:*> tool-call format are not implemented.
    
    * Muse-Glimmer: let callers cap the image token budget
    
    The image token budget is the dominant cost before the first token, and as
    committed it was unreachable: `preprocess` overwrote the caller's
    `UserInput.Processing.resize`, and `MuseGlimmerProcessorConfiguration` had no
    public initializer, so 4096 merged tokens was effectively hard-coded.
    
    Measured on an M4 Max, a 2400x2400 image at the checkpoint's 4096 budget
    expands to 4158 prompt tokens and takes 26.6 s to the first token. At 1024 it
    is 1086 tokens and 7.2 s. The budget matters more than it first appears
    because it is not only ViT work: each merged token becomes a prompt token that
    must be prefilled through all 52 text layers, and prefill runs at roughly
    200 tok/s. Text-only control runs confirm the split -- 965 text tokens take
    4.3 s and 4205 take 19 s with no image involved at all.
    
    Two ways to lower it:
    
    - `UserInput.Processing.maxPixels`, the existing model-agnostic per-call
      resize budget. One merged token covers `patch_size * merge_size` squared =
      784 pixels, so the two budgets convert directly.
    - `MuseGlimmerProcessorConfiguration(imageProcessor: .init(maxImageTokens:))`
      for a request-independent default.
    
    A caller-supplied `resize` now bounds the source dimensions instead of being
    discarded. It cannot set the output size directly, since that has to land on
    the patch/merge grid, and it will not upscale.
    
    Defaults are unchanged: with no `maxPixels` and no `resize`, the same image
    still produces an identical grid and token count, and the reference-parity
    check is unaffected.
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>

